{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 630,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time I finish this sentence, up to a billion billion dark matter particles may have streamed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5370,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through your body like ghosts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7520,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particle or particles of the dark sector\nmake up the vast majority of the mass in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11930,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe - so to them, you are the ghostly one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15860,
    "dDurationMs": 2344,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we're going to try to make contact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23160,
    "dDurationMs": 3879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see the influence of dark matter in the\norbits of stars and galaxies, in way light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27039,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bends around galaxies and clusters, in the\nclumpiness of the cosmic background radiation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32489,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33489,
    "dDurationMs": 5601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s become disturbingly clear that we can’t\nsee around 80% of the matter in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39090,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even more disturbing is that there doesn’t\neven seem to be a candidate for dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43250,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the known family of particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45090,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re faced with the eerie reality of the\ndark sector - perhaps there’s an entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50350,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "family of particles that exists in parallel\nto those we can see - a dark universe that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55760,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overlaps our own, but so far is hidden from\neven our most ingenious experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61850,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to open the gateway to\nthe dark sector and see what we can find."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67110,
    "dDurationMs": 4189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we talk about the “dark sector” we\ntypically mean a particle or family of particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71299,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that contribute to dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73179,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it’s possible that dark matter is not\nparticles - it could be black holes or failed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77850,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars or even weirder so-called “compact\nobjects”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80560,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It might even be that what we perceive as\ndark matter is really a glitch in the laws"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85649,
    "dDurationMs": 1451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we use to describe gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87100,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those possibilities are for another time\n- today we’re focusing on the bizarre physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92570,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the dark sector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93570,
    "dDurationMs": 2909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s begin with what we do know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96479,
    "dDurationMs": 4051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our best understanding of the subatomic world is given by the Standard Model - which describes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100530,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the behavior of the known family of particles with incredible success."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104869,
    "dDurationMs": 4552,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The visible universe is made of these particles, interacting with each other through the standard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109421,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model forces - the strong and weak nuclear\nforces and electromagnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114200,
    "dDurationMs": 1629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plus gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115829,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, the behavior of a particle is\ndetermined by the forces it interacts with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120549,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of forces as the languages that particles use to communicate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125840,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any electrically charged particle experiences the electromagnetic force and can communicate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130619,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with other charged particles by exchanging\nphotons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134750,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for an electrically neutral particle like\na neutrino, electromagnetism is a language"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139780,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it doesn’t speak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141630,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutrinos are unaffected by that force, and\nso they are quite literally invisible to photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147220,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A more technical way to think about this stuff is in terms of quantum fields - where each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151360,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle and force is a vibration in its own\nfield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154890,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These fields fill the universe, overlapping\neach other - and if a particle field is connected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159630,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to - coupled with a force field then it can\nspeak the language of that force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165380,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The force of gravity is a sort of lingua franca,\na common language that every particle with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170530,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass can speak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171750,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But gravity is a little different to the other\nforces - it’s not part of the Standard Model,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175610,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we don’t even know if it has a quantum\nfield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178380,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main requirement for a dark matter particle is that it doesn’t “speak electromagnetism”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183160,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t produce light - hence the “dark”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185600,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also doesn’t absorb light - otherwise\nwe’d be able to detect it when it blocked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189580,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light from the more distant universe - in\nthe same way we “see” the black lanes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194620,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dust that block the light from the center\nof our galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197850,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, dark matter is both perfectly dark AND\nperfectly transparent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202370,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Good - so it must be electrically neutral\nlike the neutrino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207960,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter can’t have charge but it must\nhave mass because the only thing we’ve ever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214290,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually seen dark matter do is to exert its\ngravitational influence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218640,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter “speaks gravity”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220700,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we can learn an awful lot from HOW it\nexerts its gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225900,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can map where dark matter is found by how it affects the rotation of galaxies, and how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230840,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it drives the orbits of galaxies inside galaxy\nclusters, and by the way it bends light around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236030,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies and clusters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238360,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These tell us something really important:\ndark matter is far more spread out - more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243260,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse - than almost all of the visible matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247100,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that tells us a lot about any prospective\ndark matter particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250030,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, dark matter doesn’t tend\nto interact with itself - at least not very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254490,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255490,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it did, then giant regions of dark matter\nwould lose energy in those collisions and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contract."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261330,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They might collapse into dark matter galaxies or dark matter stars or dark matter people."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265850,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But no - dark matter seems to stay puffed\nup in gigantic halos surrounding the much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271780,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more concentrated clumps of visible matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274040,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, galaxies are really just shiny dustings of stars, sprinkled deep in the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279570,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wells of massive reservoirs of dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282660,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the fact that dark matter forms those\ngiant halos at all tells us something very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287540,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288540,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It gives dark matter a temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291220,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More accurately, it tells us how far dark\nmatter particles were able to travel in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296210,
    "dDurationMs": 1390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297600,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This “free-streaming length” of dark matter\nis how far a dark matter particle could travel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302440,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before interacting with something - typically \nanother such particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306310,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early universe, that distance influenced the size of the seed structures which galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311750,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would later form from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313300,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about that structure formation\nbefore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, based on how that structure did end up forming, it seems likely that dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320520,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was moving pretty slowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322700,
    "dDurationMs": 2969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We refer to such dark matter as “cold”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325669,
    "dDurationMs": 4451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s review - if dark matter is a particle,\nit’s electrically neutral and doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330120,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact much with itself, and it’s relatively\nslow-moving, and also insanely abundant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335620,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a long time people thought the neutrino\nmight be dark matter - being neutral and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339760,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most abundant known particle in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341710,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the neutrinos of the standard model move too fast - they are “hot” - and there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346740,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just isn’t enough mass in neutrinos to do\nthe job, due to them being ridiculously light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352150,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s really nothing else in the standard\nmodel that works - which sounds annoying,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356870,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but actually gets physicists very excited\n- because discovering a dark matter particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362040,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be our best for finding a bigger, deeper\ntheory than the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366669,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would also be a no-brainer Nobel prize\n- and many researchers have devoted their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371180,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lives to hunting down this particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374010,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter hunters come in two breeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377050,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One type searches for new evidence out there in the universe or in our particle experiments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381050,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here on Earth for evidence of particles that\ndon’t fit the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385020,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other delves deep into theory - in speculative mathematics beyond the standard model for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390900,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "signs of new particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392740,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to focus on the theoretical prospects - because we might as well have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397340,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some fun before those pesky “observations”\nruin everything with their “facts”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402990,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, we don’t have to go too far beyond\nthe standard model to find our first dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406280,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter candidate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407810,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Completely independently of our quest for\ndark matter, physicists have hypothesized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411500,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new type of neutrino - the so-called sterile\nneutrino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414800,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I won’t describe these in detail now because we’ve been over them before - but in short:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420490,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as ghostly as neutrinos are, sterile neutrinos are far ghostlier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424470,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They don’t even interact by the weak force,\nwhich means they’re almost impossible to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428440,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429620,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some exceedingly clever experiments to do so - like we saw that time we visited"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434290,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FermiLab."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435290,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If sterile neutrinos exist AND are massive\nand slow-moving enough, they’re a great"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440449,
    "dDurationMs": 1491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter candidate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441940,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another candidate we’ve discussed is the\naxion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445480,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a weird little particle that popped\nup in the math when physicists were trying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449430,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to solve another mystery of physics - the\nso-called CP problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453540,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Axions, if they exist, would be incredibly\nlight - maybe 1% or less the mass of the already-puny"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460180,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461180,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to account for dark matter they’d need\nto exist in prodigious numbers … but according"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465220,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to pro-axion physicists, that may well be\nthe case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469020,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, enough with the things we’ve already\ntalked about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472420,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Explorations of the theoretical landscape\nhave led physicists to multiple possibilities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477040,
    "dDurationMs": 1629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for dark matter particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478669,
    "dDurationMs": 2431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the most promising ideas comes from supersymmetry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481100,
    "dDurationMs": 6569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve also talked about supersymmetry, but\nnot about how it could give us dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487669,
    "dDurationMs": 3941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supersymmetry is an extension of the standard model which proposes that all the regular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491610,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles - both matter and force-carrying\n- have twins - counterparts on the opposite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496610,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side of the table."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498030,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every matter particle or fermion has a supersymmetric force-carrier, or boson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503040,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And every boson has its fermion twin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505540,
    "dDurationMs": 3111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s expected that these supersymmetric\nparticles are much heavier than their standard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508651,
    "dDurationMs": 3378,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model counterparts - and that may explain\nwhy we haven’t seen them in our particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512029,
    "dDurationMs": 3331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerators - perhaps we just haven’t produced enough energy to make one yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515360,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they may have been produced in the insanely\nenergetic early universe, and the leftovers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519210,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from that time could still be throwing their\nweight around, so to speak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522810,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The simplest kind of dark matter we get from supersymmetry is called a ‘neutralino.’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526550,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a sort of ‘three in one particle’\nwhere the electrically neutral superpartners"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532050,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Z boson, photon, and Higgs particle,\nall mix together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535700,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some models these are the lightest supersymmetric particles possible - ”LSPs” - but they’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541740,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still incredibly heavy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543270,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while normally heavy things tend to decay to lighter things, if these can’t decay into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549860,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Standard Model particles then they’d be stable and long lived- an almost perfect dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554980,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555980,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other dark matter candidates in\ndifferent flavours of supersymmetry - all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560330,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of them “LSPs” - for example the counterparts of the neutrino or the graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566490,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expected mass of these particles is eerily\nclose to the mass expected for a certain type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571470,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of dark matter - which some would say is a\npoint in favor of supersymmetry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576560,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seeming coincidence is sometimes called “the WIMP miracle”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580980,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for that to make sense I should probably explain what a WIMP is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585660,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supersymmetric dark matter particles like\nthe neutralino are examples of a general dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589220,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter particle type called the WIMP, or “weakly\ninteracting massive particle”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594630,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of the WIMP was proposed independently of any actual WIMP candidates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599130,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a description of what some physicists\nthought dark matter particles had to be like-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604399,
    "dDurationMs": 3121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is to say, weakly interacting and massive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The massive part is obvious enough - it helps if you want to make up 80% of the mass in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612720,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe, and also slows the particle\ndown - helps make it “cold”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617839,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also covered weakly interacting - it helps dark matter halos stay puffed up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622790,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also turns out that the interaction strength of dark matter is extremely important - it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628730,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may have governed how every interesting thing in our universe first formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633740,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea is this: In the first fractions of\na second after the Big Bang, particles and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638930,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their antimatter counterparts would have been popping into existence constantly, borrowing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643740,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy from the crazy radiation of that time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646690,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then when the particle bumps into its\nantiparticle they both annihilate, releasing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651490,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that energy again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653120,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the universe cooled and energy dropped,\nthat process ceased."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657300,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We were left with a universe full of particle-antiparticle pairs that would then just annihilate over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661860,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662860,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But its possible some particles may not have been able to find an antiparticle counterpart"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668000,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before the expanding universe pulled them\ntoo far apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672440,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things like electrons and antielectrons, or\npositrons, interact very strongly via the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676800,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic force - which means they find each other too easily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680760,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe didn’t expand fast enough to\nthrow these particles apart, and so almost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684850,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all annihilated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685980,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a WIMP, with its extremely weak interaction, would more easily dodge its antimatter buddy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692020,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and so countless may have survived to this day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696540,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it turns out you can do a calculation of\nwhat interaction strength such a relic particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701750,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would need to have in order to survive in sufficient numbers to give us dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705610,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that interaction strength is about the\nsame as the weak interaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710550,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ergo, WIMPs interact by the weak force only, or something weaker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715580,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have multiple possible members of the dark sector - and we didn’t even cover"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719980,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721460,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps none exist, but perhaps several do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723670,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s possible that an entire ecosystem of\nparticles are going about their dark business"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728480,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the universe - interacting by dark\nforces, all of them oscillations in their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733060,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own dark quantum fields - perhaps with their own complexity and diversity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737870,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because dark matter is weakly-interacting,\nour light sector is probably more complex"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743850,
    "dDurationMs": 1010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- probably."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744860,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to know one way or another one of the\nmany brilliant experiments currently in progress"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750399,
    "dDurationMs": 2441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or planned will need to bear fruit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752840,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll talk more about those experiments\nanother time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755110,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, we’ll just have to enjoy knowing\nthat our light-weight light sector exists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760360,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in parallel to this completely invisible and\nvastly more massive sector of dark spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767870,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to comments go, we just wanted to let you know that while we love talking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771060,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about Space, if you have more Earthly concerns, you should check out PBS Terra on YouTube, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777130,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their show Weathered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778880,
    "dDurationMs": 1209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here is a quick trailer:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780089,
    "dDurationMs": 2567,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are you prepared for the next disaster?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782656,
    "dDurationMs": 3553,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The flood's right there. Keep going. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786209,
    "dDurationMs": 4155,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are you ready to help your family and neighbors?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790364,
    "dDurationMs": 4986,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Welcome to Weathered. In this show we'll explore the most pressing natural disasters in the US"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795350,
    "dDurationMs": 3744,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How they're changing and how you can prepare. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801503,
    "dDurationMs": 4487,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is literally a life saver. Like this saves peoples lives. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815440,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the last episode we asked the question I know all of you had on your mind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819120,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Would a spaceship traveling near the speed of light cause a closed universe to contract"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823339,
    "dDurationMs": 3021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so much that it would smash into its own ass?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826360,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the process of answering that we explored the ladder paradox and the twin paradox, and that's what our questions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831690,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are about today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832690,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cezar Catalin asks what if the ladder traveling through the barn stops when the outside observer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838970,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sees both doors closed?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840910,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does the ladder instantly expand, exploding both doors outwards?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845880,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that's awesome question - the answer looks different depending on your reference frame."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851310,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the frame of the barn, the ladder is\nfully inside and the exit door remains closed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856209,
    "dDurationMs": 2641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say it’s an adamantium barn door."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858850,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ladder collides with the door at a high fraction of the speed of light and a shockwave blasts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863510,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down the length of the ladder totally destroying it and embedding the walls of the barn with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867600,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "splinters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868600,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And all those splinters have to be inside\nthe barn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872100,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the ladder’s perspective, its butt\nwas still outside the barn when its front"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876269,
    "dDurationMs": 1431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end hit the door."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877700,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s an absolute limit to how fast that\nimpact could communicate itself down the ladder."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882870,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The shockwave can go no faster than the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885810,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So by the time the base of the ladder even\nknows that anything at all has happened to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889459,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the front of the ladder, it’s already inside\nthe barn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892209,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The shockwave hits the base of the ladder\nat the same position in the barn from either"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896500,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ladder or the barn’s point of view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899360,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Etu Suku points out that the difference between the traveling and stay-at-home twin is actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904780,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceleration, so shouldn’t acceleration\nbe used to account for the time difference?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909630,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well I’m actually glad you mentioned that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes, the traveler needs to decelerate and\nthen accelerate again to turn around, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916680,
    "dDurationMs": 4719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "according to Einstein’s equivalence principle, acceleration is fundamentally the same as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921399,
    "dDurationMs": 3601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitation as far as the laws of physics are concerned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925000,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So can we apply the gravitational time dilation to the traveling twin, as though they were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930209,
    "dDurationMs": 3511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standing on the surface of a planet with the\nsame gravitational acceleration?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933720,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes we can, and we’d get exactly the same\nanswer as if we just have the traveling twin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938250,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instantly flip its velocity around with no\nslowing down or speeding up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942070,
    "dDurationMs": 3509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that there’s no distinction\nbetween all the types of time dilation as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945579,
    "dDurationMs": 2161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far as the universe is concerned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947740,
    "dDurationMs": 4969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s something we really do need to\nexplore more carefully - in an episode coming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952709,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very very soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953709,
    "dDurationMs": 4442,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From Brandon Treu: This isn’t related to\nthis episode, however having listened to this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958151,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "show over the last year and being challenged intellectually regularly has inspired me to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962730,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go to college. I had my first class today!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965790,
    "dDurationMs": 2789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well Brandon, thank YOU for sharing this!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968579,
    "dDurationMs": 3601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is exactly the kind of feedback that\nkeeps us doing the show."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972180,
    "dDurationMs": 4171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we played a small part to inspire you to\nembark on this new adventure then it’s all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976351,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been worth it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977351,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But really, it sounds like you’re the kind\nof guy who seeks inspiration and runs with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981050,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it - the the credit’s yours my friend."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983900,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a final thought from Sebastian Elytron,\nwho expresses surprise that apparently Einstein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988459,
    "dDurationMs": 1221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a real person."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989680,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He always thought he was a theoretical physicist."
